  "id": "Compound::DB01239 - Gene::1129",
  "verbalization": "Chlorprothixene appears to interact with Chrm2 through a shared pathway involving muscarinic acetylcholine receptors. The evidence suggests that Chlorprothixene binds to Chrm5, which participates in muscarinic acetylcholine receptor activity, and this activity is linked to Chrm2. Similarly, Chlorprothixene binds to Chrm4, which is involved in G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity, also leading to Chrm2. These findings indicate that Chlorprothixene may influence Chrm2 through different receptor pathways, both involving acetylcholine receptor activity. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential mechanism by which Chlorprothixene modulates Chrm2, possibly through the activation of muscarinic or G-protein coupled receptors. The overlapping involvement of Chrm2 in both muscarinic and G-protein coupled receptor activities suggests a broader role in acetylcholine signaling, which may be a key factor in the drug's effect on Chrm2.",
